  -> CONTROL: the randomly initialised checkpoint has ignition 1.00 and lambda +0.3598. IT DOES NOT LOOK UNTRAINED, so the revision may not be what its name says and nothing below is trusted. KILL: no crossing on this grid. The trained checkpoints do not put lambda_ca above zero, so on the evidence available the transition is Pythia-specific and the oldest objection in the project is confirmed rather than answered. BOUNDARY, REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN AND BINDING ON HOW THIS MAY BE WRITTEN. This generalises the PHENOMENON, not its TIMING, and the reason is the field's checkpoint supply rather than a design choice. Pythia-410m crosses within 0.54-1.07B tokens; OLMo-2's grid has one point at 1B and the next at 21B, so any crossing here is bracketed to a ~20B-wide interval -- forty times wider. OLMo-1B has ZERO checkpoints inside Pythia's window (earliest 4B, spacing 4B), TinyLlama publishes a single branch, and Amber is 7B parameters at ~3.6B spacing. Pythia is an outlier in early-checkpoint density, and 'is the transition at the same token count across families' is therefore NOT ANSWERABLE with public checkpoints today. That is a fact about what can be known, and it is the honest residue of this debt. ATTRIBUTION: tokenizer, architecture, corpus, data order and optimiser all differ from Pythia simultaneously. This is a generality test and cannot attribute any difference it finds to any one of them.

